.TH BASHIFY 1 "2026-07-30" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
bashify \- state a project's promise as a contract a shell can hold
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bashify
[\fIlist\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify emit
\fIproject\fR \fIverb\fR \fIsummary\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify coin
\fIproject\fR \fIverb\fR \fIsummary\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify page
\fIverb\fR \fIcommand\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify check
\fIpage\fR \fIcommand\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify amend
\fIpage\fR
.br
.B bashify
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bashify
is the verb of
.BR basheur .
To bashify a thing is to write down what it promises, in a form a shell can
check, so that the general intelligence standing in for it becomes
unnecessary.
.PP
The unit of work is a
.BR "man page" .
Not as documentation of a finished tool \- as the
.B contract
the tool is then obliged to satisfy. A page is written before its utility is
complete, and the utility is judged against the page rather than the reverse.
This is why
.B bashify
is the only utility in this family whose own subject matter is man pages: it
writes the promises the others are held to, and it is held to one itself.
.PP
.PP
.B "One noun, many verbs."
A project is a noun; a noun does several things. The first pass through this
family gave each project exactly one verb, and that was the pass's shortcut
rather than a decision \- it broke wherever a project did more than one thing,
welding unrelated domains into a single verb's subcommand table. A
.B bashified
branch is therefore expected to carry SEVERAL verbs, and the acts below divide
accordingly: one bootstraps a branch, another adds to a branch that already
exists. A branch with verbs on it is the normal case, not an odd one.
.PP
Five acts, in the order they occur:
.TP
.B emit
BOOTSTRAP a branch that does not exist yet. Read a project's real repository,
discover the tooling that exists, and produce a
.B bashified
branch carrying a verb\-named utility, its man page, its contract, and a
contract test. It rebuilds the branch from scratch, beginning by removing
everything on it. Against a branch that already carries more than one verb it
refuses as a usage error and names
.B coin
instead: a rebuild there destroys hand\-written verbs, and destruction is not
regeneration. The branch is built in a
.BR git\-worktree (1),
so a project's working tree is never touched, even mid\-session. Discovery
reads every tracked executable program anywhere in the tree, not only
.IR bin/ ,
and not only those written in the shell, because a project that keeps its
tooling elsewhere \- or writes it in another language \- otherwise ships a
utility silently missing most of itself. "Script" here means any program a
caller runs, whatever it is written in: a subcommand count that tracks one
language is a front door that reports the tree it can read as the tree that
exists.
.PP
The summary
.B emit
writes for a verb is one clause. Row 1 of
.B THE PAGE TEST
rejects an "and" in a NAME line, so a generator that emits one has written a
page that fails its own contract before any implementation exists.
.TP
.B coin
ADD one verb to a branch that already carries verbs. It writes
.IR bin/ \fIverb\fR
and nothing else: it touches no verb already present, and it sources the
branch's OWN runtime rather than the skeleton's, so a coined verb speaks the
same exit vocabulary as its neighbours in the same
.IR bin/ .
A coined verb wraps no legacy script, because it is named before an
implementation exists; every subcommand it declares reports GAP until built at
its own call site. It writes no man page \- that is
.BR page ,
against the live command, which is the order this family documents.
.TP
.B page
Write or rewrite the man page for one verb, standalone, against a live
command.
.TP
.B check
Score a man page against the nine\-row page test and report the rows that
fail, the limits of the run, and the exit codes it could not provoke. Exits
.B 7
when the page fails a row: the page failing is not this utility failing, so
it is not reported as 5. See
.B THE PAGE TEST
below.
.TP
.B amend
Change a promise. This is a different act from keeping one, and it is gated;
see
.B FULFILLING VERSUS AMENDING
below.
.PP
Run
.B bashify list
to see how each is backed here and now.
.B MECHANIZED
runs free with no model in the loop.
.B SUMMON
is a promise kept by judgment rather than by lookup: it refuses with
.B 3
until
.B \-\-summon
authorises the spending.
.B GAP
is neither \- no tooling and no escalation \- and exits
.B 4
naming what is missing. None of the three ever exits 0 having done nothing.
.SH THE PAGE TEST
.B bashify check
scores nine rows. A page passes when a reader with no access to the
repository can predict the tool's behaviour from the page alone, and every
one of those predictions is machine\-checkable.
.TP
.B 1 NAME
One clause. An "and" in the NAME line means the utility is two utilities.
.TP
.B 2 SYNOPSIS
Every form shown runs as written.
.TP
.B 3 SURFACE
Bidirectional. Every flag and subcommand on the page exists; every flag and
subcommand that exists is on the page. An undocumented surface fails as hard
as a documented ghost.
.TP
.B 4 EXIT
Every code the page lists is reachable by some invocation, and every code the
tool can return is listed.
.TP
.B 5 EXAMPLES
Doctests. Each is executed and its stated output matches. An illustrative
example is a falsehood with a shell prompt in front of it.
.TP
.B 6 COST
Whether the tool can spend money is answerable from the page alone, and
.I which
of its subcommands spend is answerable too. Silence fails. Naming
.B \-\-summon
once at the top of a page is not enough when most verbs carry it: the flag
sorts nothing if the answer is always yes, so the page states the spending
subcommands by name.
.TP
.B 7 LINEAGE
SEE ALSO names the standard tools this one behaves like, so expectations can
be transferred instead of a dialect learned.
.TP
.B 8 PURGE
No vendor and no external paid service is named anywhere on the page, in its
examples, or in the paths it prints.
.TP
.B 9 TENSE
Present tense only. The page states what is. What is merely intended belongs
in
.I GAPS.md
and exits 4 at the call site.
.SH FULFILLING VERSUS AMENDING
Keeping a promise and changing a promise are different acts with different
gates, and conflating them is how a contract stops meaning anything.
.PP
.B Fulfilling
leaves the page byte\-identical and moves the implementation toward it. It
needs no permission and is what most work is. Success is rows going from fail
to pass with the page untouched.
.PP
.B Amending
changes the page, so it changes the promise.
.B bashify amend
gates it on four things: a stated reason naming what the tool learned that
the page did not know; the previous page preserved in version control rather
than overwritten in place; a full re\-run of the nine rows against the new
text; and a search for callers of the verb across bashified branches, since a
changed promise breaks a downstream pipeline silently.
.PP
The failure this gate exists to catch is amending a contract to match a
broken implementation. If the only thing that changed is that the tool cannot
do what it said, that is exit
.B 4
and a line in
.IR GAPS.md ,
not an edit to the page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-summon
Authorise spending real money. Accepted only by a subcommand that spends \-
today
.B page
alone \- and a usage error anywhere else, because a cost flag that exits 0
having done nothing is an implicit spend wearing a no\-op. It authorises; it
does not request. There is no short form; see
.B THE COST BOUNDARY
below.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output. Honoured by
.BR list .
Requested anywhere else it exits 4 rather than being ignored, because a flag
that is silently dropped is a caller lied to.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed. Honoured everywhere.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of flags; everything after is a positional argument.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
.B bashify spends money, through one front door, and only under \-\-summon.
One subcommand spends:
.BR page ,
because writing a page states a promise no tool yet keeps, and there is no
mechanical source to derive that from. Every other act is a shell reading
files. Behind the door stands a contract store, which meters the spending and
prints the cost before any is incurred.
.PP
This reverses the boundary as first written, and the reason is a change in
what
.I animate
means here rather than a change of policy about money. A thing is animate
when it carries an agenda of its own \- a loop, an initiative, a nightly pass
it launches unbidden. Reaching a general intelligence is not that. A verb that
summons at a call site, because a caller typed the word, does not house one;
it calls one. The law forbidding a project to grow its own is untouched.
.PP
The cost of the reinterpretation is real and is stated here rather than
discovered later: when almost every verb can spend, the presence of
.B \-\-summon
stops sorting anything, and
.B \-\-help
answers "can this cost me?" with yes nearly everywhere. So the question that
carries the information moves from whether a tool spends to
.I which of its subcommands
do, which is why the page names them and why row 6 of the page test requires
it.
.PP
There is deliberately no short form:
.B \-s
collides with existing tools and
.B \-S
differs from it by one shift key, which is an unacceptable property for the
only flag that costs real money. Spelling the word out is the deliberateness.
.B \-\-help
names the flag and its cost, and
.B list
names which subcommands reach it, so the two together answer "what can cost me
anything?" \- which is the question worth asking once the answer to "can it" is
usually yes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BASHIFY_WORK
Directory in which
.B emit
builds its worktrees. Defaults to a scratch path.
.TP
.B BASHIFY_REPO
The real repository, when a project lives inside a larger one it does not own
and its registered path holds no repository of its own.
.TP
.B BASHIFY_SCOPE
Limits discovery to one subtree, so a pass can never claim files belonging to
a co\-owner of a shared repository.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. Authorising spending with no act attached is
one of these.
.TP
.B 3
NEEDS\-SUMMON: the promise can be kept, but only by judgment, and the spending
was not authorised. A finding rather than an error \- the subcommand names the
purpose of the spending and its cost, then refuses.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the tooling to keep this promise does not exist yet, and no escalation
exists either. The subcommand names what is missing on standard error.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran with valid arguments and failed to finish.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read what it was asked to judge \- a page that does not
exist, or a subject that is not executable. This is "I cannot see", never
"nothing to report", and it is never reported as a pass.
.TP
.B 7
The page under test failed at least one row. Specific to
.BR check ,
and declared above the shared vocabulary rather than reusing one of its
codes, because the subject failing is not the tool failing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/bashify
This utility.
.TP
.I bashify.sh
The implementation
.B emit
runs.
.TP
.I lib/coin.sh
The implementation
.B coin
runs. It refuses rather than writes when the branch is absent, the verb name
is taken, or the working tree is dirty \- a coin landing on uncommitted work
cannot be told apart from it.
.TP
.I lib/check.sh
The page test
.B check
runs.
.TP
.I lib/amend.sh
The four\-step gate
.B amend
runs. It rules on an edit; it never writes one, because a gate that also
writes can be satisfied by its own output.
.TP
.I skel/lib/verb.sh
The shared runtime: one copy of the argument grammar, the cost boundary, and
the exit vocabulary, so utilities cannot drift into dialects.
.TP
.I skel/test/contract-test.sh
The contract test. It takes any command, so the same assertions run against a
legacy implementation and its replacement.
.TP
.I GAPS.md
What this page names and the tooling cannot yet do.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the subcommands and whether each is backed by tooling:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify list
MECHANIZED    amend
MECHANIZED    check
MECHANIZED    coin
MECHANIZED    emit
MECHANIZED    list
SUMMON        page
.fi
.RE
.PP
Score a page. The page and the command it describes are both named, so one
page can be scored against a legacy implementation and its replacement:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify check man/bashify.1
bashify: check takes exactly 2 arguments: <page> <command> (got 1)
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask for the one act that spends, without authorising the spending. It refuses
and prices itself rather than either failing silently or charging:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify page dose bin/dose
bashify: this needs a summon: writing a man page states a promise no tool yet keeps; that is judgment, not lookup
bashify: no mechanism for it exists yet, so nothing was done and nothing was spent.
bashify: cost if summoned: unmeasured; metered by the contract store, printed before spending
bashify: re-run with --summon to have an agent perform it AND leave behind
bashify: the mechanism that performs it without an agent next time.
$ echo $?
3
.fi
.RE
.PP
Authorise spending with no act attached. The flag grants permission; it never
requests work, so on its own it is a usage error rather than a silent no\-op:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify --summon
bashify: --summon authorises spending, but 'list' never spends. No act was requested.
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask for machine\-readable output from a subcommand that does not produce it.
The flag is refused rather than dropped:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify page dose bin/dose --json
bashify: GAP: --json: not wired for this subcommand
bashify: no tooling exists for this yet; see GAPS.md
$ echo $?
4
.fi
.RE
.SH HISTORY
Written 2026-07-30, before three of the four subcommands it describes
existed, which is the method rather than an oversight: the page is the
specification, and
.B list
reports which parts of it are specification only.
.PP
Amended the same day, when
.B check
was built at its own exit\-4 call site and scored this page 6 of 9. The
amendment records what the tooling learned that the page did not know: that a
scorer needs an exit code meaning "the subject failed", that a form carrying
placeholder arguments has to declare itself unrunnable, and that two of the
examples had gone false the moment
.B check
started working. The previous text is in version control.
.PP
Amended again 2026\-07\-30, when
.B amend
was built at its own exit\-4 call site and became the first gate this page
passed through rather than around. The change is the cost boundary, reversed:
what
.I animate
means was narrowed to carrying an agenda, so summoning at a call site stopped
counting as being animate, and the one front door became defensible. The gate
refused the amendment twice before allowing it \- once for an undocumented
flag and two doctests that had gone stale, once because the new prose named
the very thing the page forbids naming. Both were the tooling catching its
author, which is the argument for the gate being a script rather than a habit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR git-worktree (1),
.BR make (1),
.BR test (1),
.BR getopt (1)
.PP
Lineage: the exit vocabulary follows
.BR test (1)
in reserving 0 for success and giving distinct nonzero codes distinct
meanings; the flag grammar follows
.BR getopt (1)
conventions including
.B \-\-
as end\-of\-flags; the specification\-before\-implementation posture is
.BR make (1)-like
in that the page states a target and the tooling reports what it cannot yet
build.
